nd hit enemies at range with  dark magic which is a new element in the  game by the way that leeches enemies  health and gives it back to you whenever  you cast a spell your demi lich will  cast a hellish blast which chases down  enemies and hits them with an area  attack using the same element that you  just used early on the demi lich is  mostly just good for cracking occasional  jokes but eventually you can stack  certain upgrades to improve its efficacy  some skills will even grant you more  companions with specific perks  increasing your own damage output based  on how many companions you've got with  you at any given time kind of messed up  but you can even use your demi lich as  bait or damage that would normally go to  you becomes redirected to your  companion's health bar instead every  class has two main action skills in the  grave born we start out with dire  sacrifice  if you find the grave mourn to be kind  of barren drive so far with its  abilities dire sacrifice is the one  action skill that looks pretty rad the  way that it works is you sacrifice some  health to deal dark magic damage but you  also steal health from all enemies  nearby the more health that you  sacrifice the more bonus damage the  skill is perfect for when you are  surrounded by lots of small enemies or  you have a large boss down to a sliver  of health and want to go in for the kill  conversely if you want a strong opening  attack you can use it as a primer and  then combine certain spells or even  leeching weapons to quickly regain any  health that you had lost the second  action skill is reaper of bones reaper  of bones is basically a game of  extending the activation timer for as  long as you can when this action skill  is cast you get an increasing bonus dark  magic damage which can heal you and  extend the duration of this attack as  long as you keep getting kills the catch  is you are constantly losing health with  seemingly no end when you run out of  health you become invulnerable for a  couple of seconds and then you come back  with a small amount of health if the  timer runs out and enemies are still  alive by the time you've run out of  health then gg my friend because you are  most likely going down combining this  with health stealing magic and guns is a  must in case you deal with enemies that  were unexpectedly tankier than your  usual skeleton archer the grave horns  also got a nice set of passive skills so  the passive skill tree mostly focuses on  leeching more health and outputting more  damage to keep you alive in those full  send moments there are some fun things  that you can unlock later down the tree  such as one particular skill that has a  chance to summon a dark hydra but the  majority of your upgrade points will be  invested into maximizing your dark magic  life stealing reducing your cooldowns or  increasing the damage output of your  companions so you decided to go  graveborn perfect great choice now let's  talk about the graveborn builds when you  choose your class you can pick an origin  story that gives you certain buffs and  debuffs to particular attributes  graveborn players should go with the  failed monk origin story which depletes  your starting strength and dexterity but  dramatically improves upon intelligence  and wisdom in terms of upgrade paths a  specific skill that you should look into  early on is the faithful thralls perk  which buffs your damage based on how  many active companions you have fighting  on your behalf most of the skills are  under the hood improvements that can  activate on kills or are passively  active in the background graveborn's  usefulness overall though is heightened  by your companions so prioritizing those  that spawn more companions on your side  are a must you'll also want to consider  which action skill that you prefer do  you gamble on the reaper of bones if so  definitely put on a health leeching  loadout i cannot stress this enough this  means focusing on weapons spells and any  gear that gives you more efficient  health regeneration this can apply to  dire sacrifice as well although there's  more room to venture out and experiment  with different elemental weapons but i  would not recommend completely  abandoning health leaching weapons or  gear from your arsenal later on in the  story you will have the option to create  multi-classes that means combining two  different classes together to create  this like awesome frankenstein fusion of  the two so you might be wondering what  second class would pair well with  graveborn well combining graveborn with  spore warden creates the  horticulturalist multi-class and the two  in my opinion work together beautifully  after all the more companions that  graveborn has the better because they're  constantly causing chaos distracting  enemies and ideally leeching health back  to you this makes spore warden an  excellent choice since it comes with a  little mushroom companion right off the  bat if i found that the action skills of  reaper of bones or diary sacrifice were  too risky especially in more difficult  situations swapping the spore warden's  barrage attack of bows was a nice change  of pace but primarily it's the mushroom  buddy that does the heavy lifting you  might be tempted to try stabilmanster as  the secondary class to combine with the  graveborn which is fine but it's  important to consider the main stats  that go into each class remember that  failed monk origin story stablemanster  tends to benefit from greater dexterity  and even strain which are not the main  focus of grave1  graveborn players are better off  leveling up constitution wisdom and even  intelligence for faster spell casting  cooldowns which may combine nicely with  spellshot if you decide that spore  warden isn't your forte  alright moving on to the stabbomancer  the stab em answer is the first class  that appears when you see the class menu  and that is no coincidence it is the de  facto solo or starter class and great  for beginners or people who haven't  played borderlands in a long time  built to deal frequent bonus damage  playing as a stab-o-mancer can almost  feel like taking a co-op partner with  you early skill unlocks focus on melee  attacks movement speed buffs and  amplified critical hit damage  collectively that gives the wonderland  stablemancer a swift and deadly skill  set that makes dungeon crawling on your  lonesome more manageable than with other  classes the stablemancer's class feed is  dirty fighting which gives the class a  higher critical hit chance than the rest  that's something that can quickly be  improved upon thanks to some crit buffs  available at the base of the skill tree  like other classes the stablemancer's  two starting action skills are built  with different situations in mind using  ghostblade the stablemancer will unleash  a massive spinning blade made of magic i  guess that does several seconds of area  of effect damage at a rate determined by  their equipped melee weapon if you'd  rather get sneaky than stabby you should  consider the stab manser's other action  skill from the shadows with this the  staba manser will go invisible for a few  seconds during which time all damage you  deal counts as a critical hit though a  less powerful crit than you'd have when  not in stealth  still the bonus damage plus invisibility  can be the deciding factor in  vanquishing a fierce pack of foes or  toppling a tricky boss you could even  just use it to escape a dicey situation  and get your fate maker out of harm's  way the stablemancer's passive skills  largely focus on buffing critical hit  damage critical hit chances and the  severity and longevity of status effects  when selecting your origin story which  is called the twist of fate the game's  starter builds consider whether you want  to make your strengths even stronger or  patch up weaknesses if you're playing  solo you should design a character  background that doesn't hurt your  constitution however the best preset in  this case is the failed monk which is  built mainly for magic casters  although keep in mind the failed monk  does give a slight debuff to your  dexterity so consider that with this in  mind the route that i went was to select  raised by elves which gives me above  average dexterity in this case and thus  even more crit damage at the cost of my  constitution but then patched up that  blemish by pouring my 10 freestanding  extra points back into constitution and  strength to even it out in the end this  made my already impressive critical hits  that much stronger without sacrificing  my own health pool as a solo player when  selecting my multi-class for wonderlands  i was a few hours into the game and  feeling like my gameplay wasn't  distinctive enough from just another  borderlands i was promised fantasy but  the weapons tended to feel like  borderlands guns in a witch's hat so to  speak  so i went with something that would give  me a new perspective on the game's  combat spellshot while my build wasn't  yet suited for becoming a part-time  conjurer i started pouring hero points  into intelligence which increased my  spell cooldowns giving me more chances  to fling magic around the battlefield  for me this made a world of difference  no longer was i just swapping among a  sea of guns that didn't really feel at  home in a fantasy game suddenly with  multi-classing i was firing critical  hits from one hand and well literal fire  from the other if you're a tried and  true borderlands fan looking to mix it  up in the spin-off adding some magic to  your repertoire makes for a well-rounded  but still quite literally sharp fate  maker  and that should get you started remember  these are just some of our suggestions  from our review build of the game you'll  probably have your own favorites so let  us know what insane builds you intend to  make and what mistakes you made along  the way make sure to check out our full  review of tiny hina's wonderlands over  on gamestop's youtube channel thanks for  watching  you may be  pungent but there's no one i'd rather  have at my side

