at boosts spell damage but  stops your shields from recharging  automatically again make sure you fully  understand spell waving stacks or skip  the skill for now because you will die a  lot when it comes to building a spell  shot i thought it sounded like fun to  fire spells as fast as possible and  cover wide areas of the field in  elemental damage you may want to focus  on more powerful spells fired less often  for the fastest rate of spellcasting  under twist of fate i went the failed  monk route to boost intelligence and  wisdom which reduces spell cooldown and  increases elemental damage at the cost  of critical hits i then spent most of  the first part of the game dumping  points into intelligence until i could  fire off spells with the smallest  possible time in between the only  negative i noticed to this build was my  melee attacks seem to be pretty weak  multi-classing the spell shot depends on  what you want to accomplish i tend to  like to do things that just feel fun but  many of you will be more focused on  maximizing your stats consider two ideas  with a ranged offensive focus glass  cannon build for a more powerful  defensive build that lets you get a lot  closer for a ranged offensive spell shot  combine them with clawbringer mostly for  the wyvern companion that can reach out  and set things on fire but also because  if you're playing with other people this  class hands out elemental team buffs as  a matter of fact a glass cannon build  behind a few friends can be insanely  powerful a great defensive spell shot  can combine their abilities with spore  warden to get a tanky mushroom buddy  that gives you space to step back and  use them sticks at a more casual pace  there's really no right answer i didn't  try this but if you go stab them answer  you can probably vastly improve your  critical damage our reviewer even  suggested going all in on the glass  cannon build by combining them with the  grave born which sounds very risky  everything you need to know about the  berserker comes from the word play that  makes up its name it is at its heart a  riff on the berserker borderlands  version of the heavy tank class with a  twist of frost focus magic one of  wonderland's new features is the  dedicated melee weapon and if you're  interested in using it often the  berserker is the perfect fit for you  combine that with the enraged mechanic  to deal bonus frost damage and you have  a powerful close range fighter that's  all about jumping into the middle of the  action if you like having a large health  pool telling your enemies to chill  and close range weapons the berserker is  for you the berserker's class feat is  rage of the ancients which activates  enraged every time you use an action  skill what does in rage do it adds bonus  frost damage to all of your attacks the  duration of base enraged lasts 15  seconds however it doesn't deplete while  an action skill is active  so if you use dreadwin which lasts 6  seconds enraged will proc at the start  giving you a total of 21 seconds you can  also increase the duration of enraged by  35  if you activate an action skill  something you can take advantage of with  feral surge your starting action skill  is dreadwind where your fatemaker will  spin around for 6 seconds dealing damage  based on the stats of their equipped  melee weapon while in effect you have  increased movement speed and immunity to  the slow status effect so you can keep  up as enemies move around and still hit  them you may notice at the start of the  game dreadwind doesn't seem all that  effective damage doesn't hit as hard as  you'd like and enemies can still attack  you and getting put into save your soul  state ends your dread wind because  drenwin is tied to the damage of your  equipped melee weapon this is a skill  that only gets better the further into  your playthrough and you want to  prioritize passive skills that boost  melee and close range damage as well as  scavenging vending machines for better  melee weapons your second skill is feral  surge a single used lunge attack that  will deal area frost damage to enemies  feral surge is a great way to get into a  group of enemies faces quickly apply  frost damage to them and then follow up  with a close range weapon like a shotgun  however the real fun comes with the fact  that you can chain feral surge as its  cooldown will reset if you kill an enemy  with it in fact feral surge will  instantly kill an enemy if their health  is below 20 percent which is pretty easy  to tell thanks to the game's segmented  health bars  if you play this right you can keep  chaining feral surge over and over  bypassing its 32 second cooldown this  pairs beautifully with enraged which as  previously mentioned can have its  duration prolonged every time you  activate a skill to truly take advantage  of feral surge requires more thought and  planning than dreadwind but if that sort  of ability chaining excites you this is  a really cool skill as you might have  guessed the berserker's passive skills  are all about increasing the power of  frost and melee damage boosting stats  like enraged and improving the health  and damage reduction so you can stay up  close and personal so the core of an  effective berserker is strong melee  damage plenty of health to keep you  alive and using your action skills as  much as possible that means allocating  points into strength constitution and  attunement for your twist of fate  village idiot is perfect for giving you  that raw damage output up front while  keeping everything else you need neutral  recovering inventory hoarder is also a  potential option as it gives you an  extra skill cool down boost but you are  going to be sacrificing some max hp and  you'll want to put your extra points  into that to even things out when it  comes to multi-classing if you decide  you want to pair berserker with another  class's action skill pick one that has a  skill you can use frequently so you're  still activating enraged all the time  for example spore warden is a great  option with barrage which can be fired  multiple times  or you could take advantage of blizzard  and use your passive frost boost to  increase the power of these cyclones  further and as mentioned the cyclones  last for a really long time and as long  as the skill is active in rage's  duration counter doesn't go down  the clawbringer is an elemental  powerhouse and perhaps the most  well-rounded of the classes with a  fistful of fire in one hand and  lightning in the other clawbringer can  bring a little bit of everything to the  table including their trusty wyvern  companion there aren't really any tricks  to clawbringer which might make it a bit  dull if you're looking to be a little  more weird or active with your class  however if you rather focus on shooting  and letting elemental damage just happen  around you with no extra effort on your  part the claw burner is a good choice  and can be a great class to pick second  and pair with any of the others the  clawbringer's class feet is wyvern  companion a cute little flying companion  that will claw out your enemy's eyes and  rain hellfire down on them any increase  to your damage also increases your  companion's damage which makes boosting  fire damage very important if you want  your wyvern to be as effective as  possible the wyverns pretty good about  just doing their own thing and as a  result it can be pretty easy to forget  they are even there if you want a feat  that feels more active than passive you  might not like the wyvern but if you  rather have a buddy that deals bonus  damage with no effort on your part  wyvern is a good choice your starting  action skill is cleansing planes a  massive hammer strike that slams the  earth or nearby enemy skulls with large  melee damage as well as a fire nova  creating a fire area of effect attack  even at the start cleansing flame is a  powerful skill and can deal some heavy  damage making it really fun and  satisfying right out of the gate  the second action skill is storm  dragon's judgment which gives the fate  maker essentially thor's hammer it's a  range skill that you throw dealing area  of effect lightning damage for 8 seconds  wherever it lands you can also recall it  back early refunding some cooldown and  yes any enemies in its path back to you  do take damage when it comes to passive  skills the clawbringer's focus is on its  three core attributes fire damage  lightning damage and their wyvern  companion rather than splitting your  points into both fire and lightning  you're better off picking one element  and focusing on it to maximize its  potential fire might seem like the  better choice because your wyvern breeze  fire however there are passive skills  that can give your wyvern lightning  damage so really it's about picking the  element based on which action skill you  prefer and which action skill you end up  picking also is going to affect how you  build your character to some degree  which is a bit of a bummer in the early  game because you do have to pick your  skill first however you can respect and  bright hoof if you change your mind  later while not quite as melee heavy as  the berserker is melee and critical  damage still play a key role in the  clobbering's action skills and as such  strength is good to invest in the class  can be quite tanky not with hp but it's  ward thanks to a few passive skills so  constitution is another place to put  points especially if you use cleansing  flame which requires you to get up close  and personal wisdom is a great option if  you want to go all in on the elemental  side of them for twist of fate you can  pick village idiot if you want to go big  on melee and crit damage or for a  slightly more risky option rogue  alchemist gives you a large boost to  status damage at the cost of  constitution this can work out well  though if you favor long range weapons  and storm dragon's judgment  when it comes to multi-classing because  of how straightforward clawbringer is  and because most of its skills don't  require extra work to activate it can  make a great second class option it's  always nice to have more elemental  damage on your attacks and pretty much  any class can benefit from having a  wyvern following you around setting  stuff on fire i found it to be a solid  pair up with the berserker who i played  as my main class i would focus on  chaining feral surge and enraged why my  wyvern would just mop up everyone else  all right y'all i'm david and i'm here  to talk to you about the grave born or  as i like to call him a demon in a  prayer now the grave warden is an  interesting class that might not be the  most attractive on paper but damn if it  isn't effective siphoning away enemy  health through dark energy is the bread  and butter of the grave horn and it's  easier said than done the key though go  all out fully sending your character  into the heat of battle barely keeping  the balance between sacrificing your  health or regaining it back is the most  effective and fun way to play graveborn  oh and you get to do it with a kind of  cute kind of creepy demon friend along  the way sweet right so in my mind the  gravemorn is an excellent class for risk  takers players who don't really want a  class that's fully reliant on the at  range spell casting cooldowns like the  spell shot but they're not interested in  the melee brawl like the berserker  either so you'd think that the graveborn  is kind of this like perfect middle  ground for an average player that wants  the best of both worlds right but the  reality is it's probably better suited  for veterans who aren't worried about a  whittling health bar and can reliably  break themselves out of a death saver  too in the heat of the battle if they go  down so let's break down the grave  borne's abilities starting with the  class feet the demi lich companion  this floating companion will follow you  around and hit enemies at range with  dark magic which is a new element in the  game by the way that leeches enemies  health and gives it back to you whenever  you cast a spell your demi lich will  cast a hellish blast which chases down  enemies and hits them with an area  attack using the same element that you  just used early on the demi lich is  mostly just good for cracking occasional  jokes but eventually you can stack  certain upgrades to improve its efficacy  some skills will even grant you more  companions with specific perks  increasing your own damage output based  on how many companions you've got with  you at any given time kind of messed up  but you can even use your demi lich as  bait or damage that would normally go to  you becomes redirected to your  companion's health bar instead every  class has two main action skills in the  grave born we start out with dire  sacrifice  if you find the grave mourn to be kind  of barren drive so far with its  abilities dire sacrifice is the one  action skill that looks pretty rad the  way that it works is you sacrifice some  health to deal dark magic damage but you  also steal health from all enemies  nearby the more health that you  sacrifice the more bonus damage the  skill is perfect for when you are  surrounded by lots of small enemies or  you have a large boss down to a sliver  of health and want to go in for the kill  conversely if you want a strong opening  attack you can use it as a primer and  then combine certain spells or even  leeching weapons to quickly regain any  health that you had lost the second  action skill is reaper of bones reaper  of bones is basically a game of  extending the activation timer for as  long as you can when this action skill  is cast you get an increasing bonus dark  magic damage which can heal you and  extend the duration of this attack as  long as you keep getting kills the catch  is you are constantly losing health with  seemingly no end when you run out of  health you become invulnerable for a  couple of seconds and then you come back  with a small amount of health if the  timer runs out and enemies are still  alive by the time you've run out of  health then gg my friend because you are  most likely going down combining this  with health stealing magic and guns is a  must in case you deal with enemies that  were unexpectedly tankier than your  usual skeleton archer the grave horns  also got a nice set of passive skills so  the passive skill tree mostly focuses on  leeching more health and outputting more  damage to keep you alive in those full  send moments there are some fun things  that you can unlock later down the tree  such as one particular skill that has a  chance to summon a dark hydra but the  majority of your upgrade points will be  invested into maximizing your dark magic  life stealing reducing your cooldowns or  increasing the damage output of your  companions so you decided to go  graveborn perfect great choice now let's  talk about the graveborn builds when you  choose your class you can pick an origin  story that gives you certain buffs and  debuffs to particular attributes  graveborn players should go with the  failed monk origin story which depletes  your starting strength and dexterity but  dramatically improves upon intelligence  and wisdom in terms of upgrade paths a  specific skill that you should look into  early on is the faithful thralls perk  which buffs your damage based on how  many active companions you have fighting  on your behalf most of the skills are  under the hood improvements that can  activate on kills or are passively  active in the background graveborn's  usefulness overall though is heightened  by your companions so prioritizing those  that spawn more companions on your side  are a must you'll also want to consider  which action skill that you prefer do  you gamble on the reaper of bones if so  definitely put on a health leeching  loadout i cannot stress this enough this  means focusing on weapons spells and any  gear that gives you more efficient  health regeneration this can apply to  dire sacrifice as well although there's  more room to venture out and experiment  with different elemental weapons but i  would not recommend completely  abandoning health leaching weapons or  gear from your arsenal later on in the  story you will have the option to create  multi-classes that means combining two  different classes together to create  this like awesome frankenstein fusion of  the two so you might be wondering what  second class would pair well with  graveborn well combining graveborn with  spore warden creates the  horticulturalist multi-class and the two  in my opinion work together beautifully  after all the more companions that  graveborn has the better because they're  constantly causing chaos distracting  enemies and ideally leeching health back  to you this makes spore warden an  excellent choice since it comes with a  little mushroom companion right off the  bat if i found that the action skills of  reaper of bones or diary sacrifice were  too risky especially in more difficult  situations swapping the spore warden's  barrage attack of bows was a nice change  of pace but primarily it's the mushroom  buddy that does the heavy lifting you  might be tempted to try stabilmanster as  the secondary class to combine with the  graveborn which is fine but it's  important to consider the main stats  that go into each class remember that  failed monk origin story stablemanster  tends to benefit from greater dexterity  and even strain which are not the main  focus of grave1  graveborn players are better off  leveling up constitution wisdom and even  intelligence for faster spell casting  cooldowns which may combine nicely with  spellshot if you decide that spore  warden isn't your forte  alright moving on to the stabbomancer  the stab em answer is the first class  that appears when you see the class menu  and that is no coincidence it is the de  facto solo or starter class and great  for beginners or people who haven't  played borderlands in a long time  built to deal frequent bonus damage  playing as a stab-o-mancer can almost  feel like taking a co-op partner with  you early skill unlocks focus on melee  attacks movement speed buffs and  amplified critical hit damage  collectively that gives the wonderland  stablemancer a swift and deadly skill  set that makes dungeon crawling on your  lonesome more manageable than with other  classes the stablemancer's class feed is  dirty fighting which gives the class a  higher critical hit chance than the rest  that's something that can quickly be  improved upon thanks to some crit buffs  available at the base of the skill tree  like other classes the stablemancer's  two starting action skills are built  with different situations in mind using  ghostblade the stablemancer will unleash  a massive spinning blade made of magic i  guess that does several seconds of area  of effect damage at a rate determined by  their equipped melee weapon if you'd  rather get sneaky than stabby you should  consider the stab manser's other action  skill from the shadows with this the  staba manser will go invisible for a few  seconds during which time all damage you  deal counts as a critical hit though a  less powerful crit than you'd have when  not in stealth  still the bonus damage plus invisibility  can be the deciding factor in  vanquishing a fierce pack of foes or  toppling a tricky boss you could even  just use it to escape a dicey situation  and get your fate maker out of harm's  way the stablemancer's passive skills  largely focus on buffing critical hit  damage critical hit chances and the  severity and longevity of status effects  when selecting your origin story which  is called the twist of fate the game's  starter builds consider whether you want  to make your strengths even stronger or  patch up weaknesses if you're playing  solo you should design a character  background that doesn't hurt your  constitution however the best preset in  this case is the failed monk which is  built mainly for magic casters  although keep in mind the failed monk  does give a slight debuff to your  dexterity so consider that with this in  mind the route that i went was to select  raised by elves which gives me above  average dexterity in this case and thus  even more crit damage at the cost of my  constitution but then patched up that  blemish by pouring my 10 freestanding  extra points back into constitution and  strength to even it out in the end this  made my already impressive critical hits  that much stronger without sacrificing  my own health pool as a solo player when  selecting my multi-class for wonderlands  i was a few hours into the game and  feeling like my gameplay wasn't  distinctive enough from just another  borderlands i was promised fantasy but  the weapons tended to feel like  borderlands guns in a witch's hat so to  speak  so i went with something that would give  me a new perspective on the game's  combat spellshot while my build wasn't  yet suited for becoming a part-time  conjurer i started pouring hero points  into intelligence which increased my  spell cooldowns giving me more chances  to fling magic around the battlefield  for me this made a world of difference  no longer was i just swapping among a  sea of guns that didn't really feel at  home in a fantasy game suddenly with  multi-classing i was firing critical  hits from one hand and well literal fire  from the other if you're a tried and  true borderlands fan looking to mix it  up in the spin-off adding some magic to  your repertoire makes for a well-rounded  but still quite literally sharp fate  maker  and that should get you started remember  these are just some of our suggestions  from our review build of the game you'll  probably have your own favorites so let  us know what insane builds you intend to  make and what mistakes you made along  the way make sure to check out our full  review of tiny hina's wonderlands over  on gamestop's youtube channel thanks for  watching  you may be  pungent but there's no one i'd rather  have at my side
 

